{
  "id": "dukotah-grid-as-key-campaign7",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Grid and onion bytes as LP2 keys",
  "question": "Do the Campaign VII A2 and B2 grid-byte and 2014 onion streams yield English under their fixed repeating-key rules?",
  "reader_question": "Could the LP2 number grid or 2014 onion hex be the key to the runic pages?",
  "summary": "Three pinned programs replay across 14,784 fixed candidate outputs. No candidate passes their English-score flag. Eight constructed-message controls validate the declared operators, with literal-F substitutions disclosed.",
  "finding": "The archived grid list, raw-token alternative bases and onion-XOR streams score at best −6.720, −6.745 and −6.597, below the source’s −5.2 flag. Four no-interrupter controls recover all 120 known runes; four interrupter controls recover 120-rune constructed messages with 3–6 literal-F substitutions each.",
  "limitations": "The archived hard-coded byte list differs from decoding raw tokens in base 60. The source score screens English-like text under repeated modulo-29 keys only; other grid readings, offsets, nonrepeating keys and languages are outside this result. Planted F-interrupter controls alter 3–6 positions of the fixed source message. The plan is retrospective; the fuller control audit was added after review. The source’s base-59 formula permits four out-of-base x digits; fitting the results into bytes does not make it valid positional base 59.",
  "next_steps": "A different grid-key claim should fix its cell reading, source bytes, key clock and complete positive control before scoring LP2.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
